.Dd November 25, 2026
.Dt NVIDIA-UVM 4
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm nvidia-uvm
.Nd NVIDIA unified virtual memory driver
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Cd "kldload nvidia-uvm"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
module provides the two device nodes the NVIDIA CUDA user-mode driver
.Pq libcuda
needs beside
.Pa /dev/nvidiactl :
.Bl -tag -width "/dev/nvidia-uvm-tools"
.It Pa /dev/nvidia-uvm
The main device.
One open file is one UVM virtual address space, bound to the address
space of the process that initialised it.
All GPU registrations, mappings and memory requests go here.
.It Pa /dev/nvidia-uvm-tools
The profiler and debugger event interface.
.El
.Pp
Both nodes are created mode 0666 when the module loads, as libcuda runs as
an ordinary user.
The module is a client of the resource manager inside the NVIDIA GPU
driver and needs an
.Pa nvidia.ko
that exports the UVM kernel interface.
.Pp
This module is the native driver of the interface: a request arrives with
the driver's own request number and is answered here.
The request numbers and the encoding of a request word are in
.In nvidia_uvm_ioctl.h ;
a native program wraps the request number in that encoding.
.Pp
Third-party Linux CUDA binaries run under the Linuxulator.
They need the optional bridge module
.Xr nvidia-uvm-linux 4 ,
which owns the Linux request numbers and translates them into the
driver's.
The driver itself depends on no Linux module and imports no Linux symbol,
so a native caller needs nothing but the driver.
.Pp
This is a clean-room implementation written from a functional
specification (revision r1), not from NVIDIA's Linux driver.
The present phase drives Blackwell GB20x GPUs
.Pq GeForce RTX 50 series
and covers GPU, GPU address space and channel registration, external
allocations
.Pq Fn cudaMalloc , Fn cudaHostAlloc ,
semaphore pools and dynamic-parallelism regions, with every GPU fault
cancelled.
Managed memory
.Pq Fn cudaMallocManaged ,
peer access, access counters and the tools event queues are later phases;
their requests answer
.Dv NV_ERR_INVALID_ADDRESS
or
.Dv NV_ERR_NOT_SUPPORTED
as the specification prescribes and log the phase at most once
every ten seconds.
The same limit applies to the message for a failed GPU lookup by UUID and
to the logged
.Va rmStatus
of a failing request, so a client that retries in a loop cannot fill the
kernel message buffer.
Other GPU generations report themselves unsupported at registration.
.Pp
The Linuxulator runs a registered ioctl handler with Giant held; that
is the business of the optional bridge module
.Xr nvidia-uvm-linux 4 ,
not of the driver.
The driver relies on Giant nowhere: its locks are
.Xr sx 9
and
.Xr mutex 9
locks of its own, and its task queues are not bound to Giant.
Giant users such as USB enumeration therefore do not wait behind a long
GPU mapping.
The loops that build page tables yield the CPU between tables, and a
mapping request stops with
.Dv NV_ERR_SIGNAL_PENDING
when the calling thread has a signal pending, after releasing the page
tables it had taken.
Unmapping does not stop early.
Page tables and the probe page are single-page DMA allocations; the
GPU DMA tag is one page in size, as
.Xr bus_dma 9
allocates the whole tag size per allocation.
.Sh SECURITY
Five requests carry an
.Va rmCtrlFd
and RM handles:
.Dv UVM_REQ_REGISTER_GPU
(with a client handle or an SMC partition reference),
.Dv UVM_REQ_REGISTER_GPU_VASPACE ,
.Dv UVM_REQ_REGISTER_CHANNEL ,
.Dv UVM_REQ_MAP_EXTERNAL_ALLOCATION
and
.Dv UVM_REQ_ALLOC_DEVICE_P2P .
The RM resolves a client handle with kernel privilege and checks nothing about
the caller, and handles are predictable (the first client of a boot is
0xC1D00000), so the module checks ownership itself, before any RM call.
The handle must be an RM client that was allocated on the
.Pa /dev/nvidiactl
file that
.Va rmCtrlFd
names in the calling process.
The descriptor must resolve with the
.Dv CAP_IOCTL
right, which is the only right required (a descriptor limited to it by
.Xr cap_rights_limit 2
is accepted; one with a limited ioctl list, see
.Xr cap_ioctls_limit 2 ,
is refused), the file must be an
nvidiactl file of
.Pa nvidia.ko ,
and
.Pa nvidia.ko
must list the handle as allocated on that file.
Allocate the root client on
.Pa /dev/nvidiactl
and pass that descriptor; a client allocated on a
.Pa /dev/nvidiaN
descriptor is always refused.
Anything else, including a closed descriptor, a file that is not nvidiactl and
another user's handle, fails with
.Dv NV_ERR_INSUFFICIENT_PERMISSIONS
in
.Va rmStatus
(0x1B; EPERM where a status becomes an errno), and RM is never called.
The client stays held in
.Pa nvidia.ko
from the check to the end of the request: closing the descriptor succeeds but
the file is not released until the request ends, and freeing the client blocks
until then, so a client that is freed and whose handle is reused by another
user cannot be picked up in between.
Holds are per client; no other client waits.
A refusal logs its cause (rate limited) to the console: an
.Va rmCtrlFd
that does not resolve, or a file that is not an nvidiactl file owning the
handle.
Holding the open file is the capability: a duplicate of the descriptor, or the
descriptor passed over a socket, is the same file and passes.
.Dv UVM_REQ_UNREGISTER_CHANNEL
names a channel in the caller's own VA space only and calls no RM function, so
it needs no check.
The check needs
.Fn nvUvmInterfaceClientHold
and
.Fn nvUvmInterfaceClientRelease
from
.Pa nvidia.ko ;
an
.Pa nvidia.ko
without them makes the module fail to load:
.Xr kldload 8
fails.
This module depends on exactly module version 2 of
.Pa nvidia.ko .
An older
.Pa nvidia.ko
makes
.Xr kldload 8
fail: the kernel linker reports a missing or mismatched dependency.
A version 2
.Pa nvidia.ko
that lacks the two functions leaves
.Dq symbol nvUvmInterfaceClientHold undefined
on the console.
The reverse fails the same way:
an older
.Pa nvidia-uvm.ko
(built against a version 1 dependency) does not load against the new
.Pa nvidia.ko .
Use the
.Pa nvidia.ko
built with the
.Pa patch-zz-nvidia_client_owner_*
patches of nvidia-gb10-freebsd.
.Sh LOADER TUNABLES
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Va hw.nvidia_uvm.builtin_tests
Built-in tests enabled; default 0, when unknown request numbers fail with
.Er ENOSYS .
.It Va hw.nvidia_uvm.enable_va_space_mm
Bind each VA space to the creating process address space; default 1.
.It Va hw.nvidia_uvm.gpfifo_entries
GPFIFO entries per UVM channel, a power of two in [32, 1048576]; default 1024.
.It Va hw.nvidia_uvm.fault_batch_count
Replayable faults fetched per batch; default 256.
.It Va hw.nvidia_uvm.wait_timeout_ms
How long a wait on GPU work may take before the module records a fatal
error; default 30000.
.It Va hw.nvidia_uvm.sysmem_gpfifo
Place the GPFIFO ring and PUT word in system memory (1, the default) or
dedicated memory (0).
On a GPU with unified memory, such as the integrated GPU of the GB10 SoC,
the GPU has no dedicated memory and uses shared system memory: the
registration succeeds, the GPU has no physical memory allocator (PMA)
object because there is no dedicated memory to allocate from, and all of
its memory is system memory.
The GPFIFO ring and the PUT word of such a GPU always go to system memory
and this tunable is ignored there, because there is no dedicated memory
to place them in.
A GPU whose frame buffer information is broken or missing is a different
case: the registration of that GPU fails with a message, and the driver
does not treat it as a GPU with unified memory.
.It Va hw.nvidia_uvm.debug_push
Log the method stream, GPFIFO entry, GP_PUT and doorbell value of the first
N pushes (at most 64), and the GPU addresses of each channel's GPFIFO,
GP_PUT, doorbell, pushbuffer and tracking semaphore; default 0 (off).
.It Va hw.nvidia_uvm.init_probe
Run channel initialisation in stages, each followed by a host-class semaphore
release and a log line, with the copy engine first used in the last stage;
default 0.
Diagnostic for a copy-engine error during the first push.
.It Va hw.nvidia_uvm.init_probe_work
With
.Va init_probe ,
a bit mask of first-work stages run on channel 0 after the initialisation
stages, each a push followed by a wait and a check of the written data:
1 virtual copy, 2 virtual memset, 4 physical system-memory copy, 8 physical
memset without flush, 16 the same with flush, 32 plus a host wait for idle,
64 plus a host membar (the push that raised Xid 71), 128 as 32 completed by a
host-class semaphore release;
256 a copy-engine semaphore release into the semaphore page, 512 a virtual copy
page to page, 1024 the same with LINE_COUNT 1, 2048 a memset with source pitch,
explicit remap and LINE_COUNT, 4096 a memset with the default words, 8192 as
2048 with a block-linear source (raises Xid 71);
16384 to 2097152 (bits 14 to 21) a second series on copy-engine write
visibility: host semaphore control, copy-engine semaphore with a copy-engine
or host marker, copy, memset, memset with host WFI and MEMBAR, and physical
memsets of a probe page.
Default 0.
Diagnostic.
.It Va hw.nvidia_uvm.ce_bind_subch4
Also bind the copy class on subchannel 4 during channel initialisation;
default 0.
Diagnostic, see spec question Q-008.
.El
.Sh SYSCTL VARIABLES
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Va hw.nvidia_uvm.fatal_error
The module-wide fatal status; 0 until an unrecoverable error (a channel
error, a timeout, a lost GPU) is recorded, after which every request fails
with it until the module is reloaded.
.It Va hw.nvidia_uvm.gpus
One line per registered GPU with its architecture, classes and fault
statistics.
.El
.Sh UNLOADING
.Xr kldunload 8
of the module fails with
.Er EBUSY
while any
.Pa /dev/nvidia-uvm
or
.Pa /dev/nvidia-uvm-tools
file is open, even one that never created a virtual address space, while a
virtual address space or a GPU registration exists, and while any mapping
of a semaphore pool or a managed range is alive.
A mapping outlives the file it was made through, because a process closes
its files before its address space is torn down, so the module stays loaded
until the last such mapping is unmapped or its process has exited.
Once an unload has begun,
.Xr open 2
of either node fails with
.Er ENXIO .
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width "/dev/nvidia-uvm-tools" -compact
.It Pa /dev/nvidia-uvm
.It Pa /dev/nvidia-uvm-tools
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr linux 4 ,
.Xr nvidia-uvm-linux 4
.Sh HISTORY
The
.Nm
driver first appeared in CloudBSD in 2026.
.Sh AUTHORS
.An REVYTECH Inc
.Sh BUGS
Only Blackwell GB20x has a hardware abstraction layer in this phase;
Ampere, Ada, Hopper and Blackwell GB100 GPUs are refused at registration.
The semaphore pool mapping cannot verify that the file offset equals the
mapped address, as the specification asks: the
.Fx
device mmap path does not pass the address to the driver.
